<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tatya Tope</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1814">1814</a>–<a href="/years/1859">1859</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Tantia Tope, Ramachandra Pandurang Tope</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tatya Tope was one of the leading commanders of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-rebellion-of-1857">Indian Rebellion of 1857</a>. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> in the service of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanaya">Nana</a> Sahib, he fought at Kanpur, joined forces with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lakshmibai">Rani Lakshmibai</a> of Jhansi and took Gwalior in 1858, then led British columns on a long <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> chase across central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Betrayed in 1859, he was tried and hanged at Shivpuri.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Tatya Tope was born Ramachandra Panduranga Yewalkar into a Marathi Deshastha Brahmin family at Yeola, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nashik">Nasik</a>. An official statement names his father as Panduranga Yewalkar and his mother as Rukhma Bai. He was a personal adherent of Nana Saheb of Bithoor. A witness at his trial described him as a man of middling stature, with a wheat complexion, who always wore a white turban. British letters were confused about him, calling him minister of Baroda in one and the same person as Nana Saheb in another.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After the rebellion at Kanpur broke out on 5 June 1857, Nana Saheb became its leader and was declared <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peshwa">Peshwa</a> in late June, after the British at Cawnpore had surrendered on 25 June. Tope acted in Nana's name from Bithur. He was one of the leaders involved in the killing on 27 June 1857 of 180 women and children after the British agreed to surrender. He held a defensive position until driven out on 16 July 1857, and was defeated again in the Second Battle of Cawnpore, which began on 19 November 1857 and lasted seventeen days, when Sir Colin Campbell counterattacked. He then took shelter with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lakshmibai">Rani of Jhansi</a>.</p><p>He helped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lakshmibai">Rani Lakshmibai</a> escape the British assault on Jhansi. Together with Rao Saheb, a nephew of Nana Saheb, they took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gwalior-fort">Gwalior Fort</a> and proclaimed Hindavi Swaraj, or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> self-rule, in the name of Nana Saheb Peshwa. After losing Gwalior, Tope and Rao Saheb fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputana">Rajputana</a>, where he persuaded the army of Tonk to join him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Tope continued resistance as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> in the jungles after the revolt was put down. He won the Siege of Charkhari against Ratan Singh and replaced artillery lost at the Banas River, then moved towards Indore with the British under General John Michel in pursuit. He and Rao Saheb divided their forces, joined again in October and were defeated at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chhota-udaipur">Chhota Udaipur</a>.</p><p>At his trial Tope admitted the charges but said he could be held accountable only to his master, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peshwa">Peshwa</a>. He was executed on 18 April 1859 at Sipri. Every year the state government and local people pay tribute to him on that day at a Shaheed Mela. Some writers, among them Srinivas Balaji Herdikar in his book The 1857, have claimed that Tope was not hanged and that another man, Narayan Rao Bhagvat, was hanged in his place.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tatya Tope” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1857 Raja Ratan Singh remained loyal to the British and was besieged by the rebel leader <b>Tantia Tope</b>, for which he was later rewarded.</blockquote><a href="/state/charkhari-state">Charkhari State</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Yeola, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. Died in Shivpuri, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=20.04,74.49&amp;year=1843" aria-label="Yeola on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Tatya Tope"><image href="/maps/globes/g060n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6152 5774h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6152 5774h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=20.04,74.49&amp;year=1843">What was here in 1843? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
